{"id": "133515", "revid": "79572", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133515", "title": "Kirkkonummi", "text": "Kirkkonummi (Swedish: \"Kyrksl\u00e4tt\") is a municipality in Uusimaa, about 30 km west of Helsinki, part of Helsinki metropolitan area. It includes the Porkkala peninsula, with nice natural landscapes and seascapes, some archipelago, the Meiko Nature Reserve and part of Nuuksio National Park. There are several lakes.\nUnderstand.\nno population data for this page Most of the municipality is traditionally Swedish-speaking, with Finnish spoken in the remote northern parts. However, with urbanisation and commuting to Helsinki, the majority has turned Finnish (75% as of 2024).\nThe old parish of Kirkkonummi was founded in the 14th century, and one of the most important historical roads in the country, the so-called \"King's Road\" between Turku and Vyborg, was laid through Kirkkonummi in the same times.\nThe southern half of the municipality was leased to the Soviet Union between 1945 and 1956 for use as a naval base as part of the peace settlement that ended the hostilities between the Soviet Union and Finland during World War II. Signs of this \"Porkkala Parenthesis\" include concrete bunkers, other fortifications and the remains of an airbase.\nGet in.\nKirkkonummi is 30 km west of Helsinki, part of the HSL regional public transport cooperation (see Helsinki#Get around; Kirkkonummi is zone D). For coaches, see Matkahuolto. The centre is half a kilometre north of highway 51 (L\u00e4nsiv\u00e4yl\u00e4, V\u00e4sterleden) between Helsinki and Raseborg. Time-wise, Kirkkonummi is 30\u201345 min away from Helsinki by car, bus or regional train.\nBy train.\nThere are HSL trains from Helsinki and Espoo: U-trains with a few stops in Kirkkonummi (Masala, Jorvas, Tolsa and the centre) mostly twice an hours and trains with fewer stops continuing westward (Y, H) mostly once an hour. The main railway station is in the south part of the centre. \nA few of the trains between Turku and Helsinki stop at. Usually you have to transfer to a regional train in Lepp\u00e4vaara, Espoo, sometimes the transfer is in Karis. In both cases, the transfer should be included on the ticket.\nBy bus.\nBuses run regularly from Helsinki and Matinkyl\u00e4, Espoo. Coaches along road 51, mostly between Helsinki and Karis but often continuing farther, stop at \"Kirkkonummenportti\" (Kyrksl\u00e4ttporten), i.e. the crossroads to the centre, and other exit ramps. The buses from Matinkyl\u00e4 (with metro access from Helsinki) usually stop at the \"travel centre\" by the Kirkkonummi rail station \u2013 don't confuse it with the bus stops \"at\" the station, used e.g. by VR coaches. Most of them make detours to other villages.\nBy car.\nKirkkonummi is a 20\u201330-minute drive from central Helsinki along highway 51, depending on the traffic. Highways 1 (E18) and 110 pass through Veikkola in the northernmost part of the municipality.\nBy bicycle.\nThe Eurovelo route EV 10 around the Baltic Sea passes through Kirkkonummi centre on its way from the Russian border via Helsinki to Turku; along this stretch it is developed and signposted. There is a in Munkinkuja just south of the railway station.\nGet around.\nThere is reasonably good public transport. See Helsinki#Get around for tickets and online timetable services of the region's public transport (HSL/HRT). The HSL trains and buses use uniform tickets (Kirkkonummi is zone D). For the odd journey, just pay with your NFC debit or credit card (use the same card to travel freely in the zone during the validity time). A single ticket is \u20ac3.50 (2026). HSL cards can be bought in the R kiosks (one at the railway station, one closer to the library).\nThe town (locals say \"byn\", \"the village\") is sufficiently small to get around in by foot: 1.2 km from Munkinm\u00e4ki to Pappilanm\u00e4ki, which covers most points of interest. However, for sights further away, the most practical mode of transportation is your own car. For islands, you may want a boat or kayak. The tip of the Porkkala peninsula is 20 km south of the centre, Veikkola 20 km north of it.\nAs of 2025, there are a lot of construction and roadworks going on in the north-eastern parts of the centre. Be prepared for detours.\nLocal bus services include:\nDo.\nThere are many opportunities for outdoor life: lake- and seashore beaches (also winter swimming available), woods and archipelago, trails for walking, jogging and biking, tracks for cross-country skiing, horse riding stables, five golf courses and a downhill skiing centre. There are also facilities for indoor skating and swimming.\nBuy.\nFor groceries, the centre has a Citymarket (in Kirsikka), a big Prisma (across the railway from Kirsikka) and a big Lidl by Munkinm\u00e4entie (see below). There are smaller grocery stores e.g. by the church and the library (a Sale, with a cobbler's, fleamarkets and Posti and Matkahuolto parcel kiosks), in Masala and by Tolsa railway stop, probably in most sub-centres.\nIn addition to those in the centre proper, there are some smaller shops along Munkinm\u00e4entie just south from the railway centre and large stores farther down the road. The latter include budget interior design (Rusta) and a hardware store, also with some interior design (K-rauta).\nConnect.\nThe main library (Fyyri) is in the northern part of the centre. There are minor libraries in Masala and Veikkola, open mostly at least from Monday to Friday 10:00-15:00 \"or\" 15:00-19:00, and a bookmobile, serving the rest of the municipality. Free Wi-Fi, public computers and scanner-printers in the libraries.\nGo next.\nEspoo to the east; to the west Siuntio and on to Inkoo, Raseborg and Hanko"}
{"id": "133516", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133516", "title": "V. S. K. Valasai", "text": "V.S.K.Valasai is a village in Dindigul district in the south of Tamil Nadu.\nGet in.\nOne can get into the village by train station located in nearby city of Dindigul, some 26 km away. Dindigul is well connected by rail link to many major cities of India such as Chennai, Bangalore, Mumbai (Bombay), and Delhi. \nV.S.K.Valasai is 27 km away from district capital Dindigul, and 56km from Madurai. The Major District Road-1111 (from Kanavaipatti to Ayyalur) is pass through V.S.K.Valasai, which connects National Highway-45 and SH-35 roads. The city is well connected by buses to all populated areas in Dindigul district via state government operated and private buses."}
{"id": "133520", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133520", "title": "Prudhoe", "text": "Prudhoe is a town in Northumberland in northeast England. With a population of 10,300 in 2021, it's the largest of a string of villages along the lower Tyne valley.\nWylam on the north bank of the Tyne has a population of 1660 but has more visitor accommodation. Other nearby villages are Ovingham and Heddon-on-the-Wall. \nUnderstand.\nThe Tyne valley is a natural transport route passing north of the Pennines towards Carlisle; Hadrian's Wall and military road followed the higher ground a few miles north of it. In the 19th century its farming villages turned into colliery and iron-working towns. Two major problems for the mines had a single solution: steam engines to pump out the workings, and to transport heavy ores and coals. George Stephenson of Wylam is the best known of several engineers from these parts, designing steam-powered locomotives. \nThese industries were small-scale compared to those further down the Tyne, and were uneconomic by the early 20th century. By then the city of Newcastle was growing and growing, so Prudhoe, Wylam and other settlements became part of the commuter belt.\nGet in.\nPrudhoe is 11 miles west of Newcastle upon Tyne on A685: leave A1(M) at jcn 73. \nTrains from Newcastle run every 30 min (and may start from Morpeth), taking 20 min via Metro Centre and continuing to Hexham, Haltwhistle (for Hadrian's Wall) and Carlisle. \n is by the Tyne bridge north side of town. It has ticket machines but no other facilities. There is step-free access to both platforms. \nSlower trains from Middlesbrough run via Stockton-on-Tees, Hartlepool, Sunderland, Newcastle and Metro Centre to Wylam and Prudhoe, continuing to Corbridge and Hexham. \n likewise has ticket machines but no other facilities. There is step-free access to both platforms.\nGo North East Bus 10 / 10B runs every 20-30 min from Newcastle Eldon Square and railway station, taking 40 min via Metro Centre, Blaydon and Ryton, and continues to Riding Mill, Corbridge and Hexham. Don't get on the 10A, which veers away south to Chopwell. The stop in Prudhoe is along Front St. \nBus 684 runs hourly from Newcastle to Heddon-on-the-Wall, Wylam, Ovingham (by Prudhoe railway station), Corbridge and Hexham.\nGet around.\nThe town is small, but you need wheels to explore the Northumbria countryside.\nTaxi operators in Prudhoe are Castle Cars (+44 1661 834800) and NT Cabs (+44 7905 299829).\n Other courses are Close House near Heddon (see Sleep), Ryton and Tyneside both near Ryton, and Stocksfield. \n Prudhoe lacks visitor accommodation, try Wylam 3 miles east. \nConnect.\nAs of Feb 2025 Prudhoe, Wylam and their approach roads have 4G (though patchy) from all UK carriers. 5G has reached A69 and Heddon on the Wall."}
{"id": "133539", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133539", "title": "Ashby-de-la-Zouch", "text": "Ashby-de-la-Zouch is a market town in Leicestershire, England. Ashby de la Zouch Castle was an important fort in the 15th to 17th centuries. \nUnderstand.\nAshby-de-la-Zouch is a small town that's best known for the company headquarters of KP Snacks. During the English Civil War it was a stronghold for the Royalists until it fell from a siege in March 1646.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nIs only 11 miles from () near Castle Donington.\nBy car.\nOff the A42 a few junctions south of the M1 making it easy to reach from Derby and Nottingham, and on the A511 between Burton upon Trent and Leicester.\nBy bus.\nBus services[dead link] available from Loughborough, Swadlincote, Burton upon Trent and Nuneaton."}
{"id": "133540", "revid": "1671335", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133540", "title": "Montgomery (Ohio)", "text": "Montgomery is in Hamilton County (Ohio).\nGet in.\nBy car.\nI-71 and US 22 pass through Montgomery.\nSleep.\nThere are no hotels in Montgomery city limits but plenty in nearby Blue Ash."}
{"id": "133636", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133636", "title": "Huntington (Oregon)", "text": "Huntington is a small town in Eastern Oregon."}
{"id": "133658", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133658", "title": "Wells-next-the-Sea", "text": "Wells-next-the-Sea is a town on the North Norfolk coast, set about 1 mi in land.\nWells' quay, often busy with fishing boats, is separated from the North Sea by a series of tidal channels and flat salt marshes. It has a series of shopping streets and is home to several quayside eateries. Wells Beach and its colourful beach huts are hidden from the town by a strip of dense pinewoods.\nThe town is enveloped by the Holkham Estate. Much of the Holkham Estate, including Holkham Hall on selected days, is open to the public.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nWells has a heritage railway station to the east of the town, in Stiffkey Road. It is the northern terminus of the Wells and Walsingham Light Railway, which is narrow gauge and typically only operates during the summer months. Services to Wells depart from Walsingham.\nThe nearest stations on the countrywide National Rail network are at:\nBy bus.\nBuses typically operate regularly during the daytimes on Monday-Saturday, with fewer services available on Sundays and bank holidays.\nRegular bus routes in Wells are:\nThe Travel Norfolk website and journey planner is run by Norfolk County Council.\nBy car.\nWells-next-the-Sea is signposted from nearby towns and villages, often with its shortened name, \"Wells\".\nThe town lies on the A149 Coast Road direct from: King's Lynn, Heacham, Hunstanton, Brancaster, Burnham Market via B1155, Blakeney, Sheringham and Cromer. Travel from King's Lynn is usually quicker using alternative routes, for example, by using the A148 then B1105.\nThe B1105 arrives in Wells direct from Fakenham.\nBy bike.\nWells is on the long-distance National Cycle Network Route 1. The route follows mainly minor roads and some off-road paths along the coastline, and is signposted:\nBy long-distance footpath.\nThe North Norfolk Coast Path, one of the National Trails, follows the county's northern North Sea coastline - from Hunstanton and Brancaster in the west and Cromer and Sheringham in the east.\nGet around.\nMost parts of Wells are accessible on foot, using pavements, off-road footpaths or shopping streets. The town is mostly flat with some shallow inclines. Some of the town's streets are very narrow.\nWells Beach is over a mile to the north of Wells Quay, accessible using off-road footpaths, with its own car park.\nConnect.\nAs of March 2022, Wells has 4G from EE, O2 and Vodafone, but no signal from Three. 5G has not reached this corner of Norfolk."}
{"id": "133672", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133672", "title": "Dartford", "text": "Dartford is a commuter town for Greater London in Kent, with a long history of religious, industrial and cultural importance town.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe Dartford tunnel and bridge are toll roads but do not have booths. Payment must be made on the website (car plate is scanned) within a couple of days otherwise you get a large fine.\nBy train.\nDomestic trains are operated by SouthEastern From London St Pancras to Ebbsfleet (typical journey time 15 \u2013 17 minutes).\nGet around.\nLocal buses are operated by Fastrack[dead link] (Arriva) and Arriva Kent Thameside[dead link]\n Ebbsfleet United (men) were relegated in 2025 to play in National League South, the sixth tier."}
{"id": "133673", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133673", "title": "Leominster (England)", "text": "Leominster (pronounced as \"Lemster\") is a town in Herefordshire, England.\nUnderstand.\nLeominster is a market town, influenced by its proximity to the Welsh border. It is located at the confluence of the River Lugg and its tributary the River Kenwater, approximately 12 miles (19 km) north of the city of Hereford and approx 7 miles south of the Shropshire border, 11 miles from Ludlow in Shropshire. With a population of approximately 11,700 people, Leominster is the largest of the five towns (Leominster, Ross-on-Wye, Ledbury, Bromyard and Kington) in the county surrounding the City of Hereford.\nGet in.\nBy train.\n is a small station offering two lines with 1 train per hour (tph).\nGo next.\nMap of places with Wikivoyage articles nearby"}
{"id": "133698", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=133698", "title": "Wauchula", "text": "Wauchula is in the Florida Heartland.\nUnderstand.\nWauchula is the county seat of Hardee County. The county courthouse is on the south side of Main Street, which is also Florida State Route 64A.\nGet in.\nWauchula can only be reached by private vehicle. There is no rail or bus service.\nGet around.\nSome taxi service is available, but mostly people travel by private vehicle in town.\nDo.\nThe Hardee County Fair is held annually at the Hardee County Fairgrounds usually February. The fairgrounds complex is also where Cattlemen's Arena and the Hardee County Civic Center are located, just west of town and south of Main Street. Through the year other community events are held in the complex.\nEat.\nThe city has various restaurants. Excluding fast food restaurants these are most of the restaurants in Wauchula:\nStay safe.\nFlorida Hospital Wauchula, at 533 Carlton Street, is in the southwest part of town. This is a small hospital that specializes in physical rehabilitation. It has some general patient rooms and it has an emergency department that was almost completely rebuilt about 2009 to 2010."}
